  "question": {
    "text": "An electron beam is accelerated by a potential difference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi></math> to hit a metallic target to produce <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>X</mi><mo>-</mo></math>rays. It produces continuous as well as characteristic <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>X</mi><mo>-</mo></math>rays. If <math><msub><mrow><mi>&#955;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></math> is the smallest possible wavelength of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>X</mi><mo>-</mo></math>ray in the spectrum, the variation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>log</mi><mfenced><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mfenced></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>log</mi><mfenced><mi>V</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;is correctly represented in :"
  },
